SECTION II. 
Related Work 
 
Most hand gesture recognition systems detect hand pose estimation to track hand landmark. When a landmark of fingertip and hand position move in the button area, it triggers a click action. Also, some special gestures are used to detect a click action. For example, fingertip holding a few seconds on the button, performing index finger, clenched fist and fingertip touching on hand palm are used [3] –​ [6] . These approaches can detect exact clicking, reduce errors and do not require additional equipment except a camera. However, some of them require continuous clicking time or hand gestures to perform. [4] , [7] propose new keyboard layout designs, which substantially improve the typing speed by cutting down unnecessary hand movements during typing. 
 
T. Lee et al. [4] produced ambidextrous keyboard design which is proposed new keyboard layout and identified the type-in action. The new keyboard layout is optimized frequency of key tapping is as close as characters. The type-in gesture is contact between the thumb and the index finger. The index finger points to the input character and remains still the same position while the contact of the thumb to the index finger makes the type-in action. 
SECTION III. 
Proposed Method 
 
Detecting the gesture to click on a button by a single RGB camera is a complicated task. We tested several different methods for detecting to click action based on finger positions, angles and movement speed. But these methods had many issues that cannot be solved smoothly. Finally, we have chosen hand typing motion for the detection. 
 
In this work, we accomplished an efficient detecting method for typing action using basic CNN. The output classifications of CNN are touch and non-touch. The overall architecture of our proposed method is shown in Figure 3 . 
